title The grid that crossed a platform -- Brushstroke's own rasterizer on AppKit, and the pump answering
prompt Keaton granted the second probe: a real cell grid through AppKit with one event coming back, carrying the seam-module shape from its first line.
think The seam came out into tools/rye/objc_seam.rye first, before a second call site existed -- every extern, the one pointer cast, the shape helper, and verify. That ordering is the point: a backend built on it never writes a cast of its own, which is the copy_disjoint discipline applied to a platform rather than to a memcpy.
think The probe imports brushstroke/skate_grid.rye through the symlink idiom brushstroke/tally_copy.rye already uses, so the rasterizer running on macOS is Brushstroke's own code unmodified. Not one glyph was redrawn. That is what makes Skate a backend beside the Wayland backend rather than a second application, and it is now shown rather than argued.
think The output half reads 16,268 lit glyph pixels on a 704-by-288 layer -- the rasterizer's own count, so the surface rendered rather than merely cleared. The input half posts a synthetic event to this process's own queue and the pump dequeues, identifies, and dispatches it; a system-level CGEventPost would have injected input into whatever window happened to be focused on somebody's machine, which a probe has no business doing.
obs The seam earned itself a third time. Of nineteen declarations it caught stringWithUTF8String: reading @@:r-star where I wrote @@:-star -- the r is const, a difference no compiler on this path would have raised and one that would have been a wrong pointer read at runtime. Eleven of twelve new guesses were right; the check exists for the twelfth.
obs Discovery mode paid for itself twice tonight: setting an encoding to "?" prints what the framework says and sends anyway, so twelve unknowns resolved in one run instead of twelve rebuild cycles. It is never left in place, and the probe asserts zero remain.
loom probe=macos_cell_grid selectors_verified=19 declarations_caught=1 grid=44x9 scale=2 surface=704x288 lit_pixels=16268 events_seen=6 prove_red_exit=134 ordinary_exit=0 swift_libs=0
file tools/rye/objc_seam.rye the one boundary, checked against the loaded framework
